// A snapped branch or collapsed trunk segment — architecture report item 9
// ("lower priority" tier), `wood`'s own `breaks_into` target once
// structural integrity (M17) covers `Plant` as well as `Solid`. Powder, not
// Plant: once it's broken free of the living tree it behaves exactly like
// any other loose debris (falls, piles at an angle of repose), not like a
// growing organism any more. Dry and disconnected from the tree's own
// moisture uptake, so more flammable than living `wood`, not less.
(
    name: "deadwood",
    display: "Deadwood",
    kind: Powder,
    density: 0.7,
    friction_angle: 38.0,
    colors: [
        (74, 50, 30),
        (86, 58, 34),
        (64, 43, 26),
        (96, 66, 40),
    ],
    flammability: 0.35,
    burn_temperature: 700.0,
    burn_duration: 300,
    burns_into: "ash",
    heat_conductivity: 0.15,
    // **A branch rots. It used to be the one thing a plant becomes that
    // never left the world again.**
    //
    // Measured 2026-08-31 on the lab bed (`examples/labmass`, 12 seeds): of
    // everything that died, **30-39% ended here and stopped**, against ~9%
    // reaching soil and ~55% rotting away. One graded cull of half the stand
    // left **963 cells of deadwood standing for the remaining 84,000 frames**
    // and for ever after. `CLAUDE.md`'s ethos section rules that shape out in
    // as many words -- an outcome is a distribution, not a binary -- and a
    // third of a dead plant's mass sitting at exactly 0% return is the
    // binary. In the lab it is worse than a loss, because the box is sealed:
    // the matter does not leave, it silts up.
    //
    // **This was an omission rather than a decision, and the difference is
    // checkable.** This file predates `Material::decays_into` -- `decay.rs`
    // was ash-only and hardcoded when M17 wrote deadwood -- and it was never
    // revisited when litter arrived and made the channel data. Every other
    // debris material argues its rates at length; this one carried no decay
    // commentary at all, and `Reports/dead-ends.md` has no entry for it, so
    // it was never tried and rejected either. The contrast that proves the
    // point is `corpse`, whose silence here *is* deliberate and is recorded
    // (dead-ends.md: *"Decay needed no hook: `corpse.ron` declares no
    // `decays_into`"*, because `EnergyLedger::meat_lost` books it instead).
    // That one stays as it is.
    //
    // **`litter`, not `soil`, and the target is the point of the change.**
    // Sending it straight to soil would return a third of the plant at a
    // stroke and teleport a snapped branch into topsoil. Sending it to litter
    // makes a *chain* -- wood -> deadwood -> litter -> soil, four states at
    // four rates -- so a branch pile visibly crumbles to leaf mould before
    // any of it is ground, which is the middle the ethos asks for. It also
    // closes the creature half: `litter` is on `ant.ron`'s food list and
    // `deadwood` is not, so a fallen branch becomes food eventually instead
    // of never.
    decays_into: "litter",
    // **1.0, the default, and deliberately not a second loss.** The mass
    // charge belongs once, at the terminal `litter -> soil` step where
    // `litter.ron` argues it against real humification efficiency. Charging a
    // fraction at every hop would make a long chain arbitrarily lossy, which
    // is a property of the chain's length rather than of anything physical --
    // `deadleaf -> litter` is 1.0 for the same reason.
    //
    // Note for anyone reading a counter downstream of this: a decay that
    // produces litter scores `World::rotted_to_solid` while returning no soil
    // at all, which is what `rotted_onward` was split out to say. Reading the
    // raw total as soil production overstated the return fourfold.
    // A branch, so: slower than a leaf and faster than a bole. `litter` runs
    // 0.5/0.1 and `log` 0.02/0.002; these sit between, nearer the log. At
    // `decay::DECAY_TICK_INTERVAL` of 200 frames the dry rate is a mean
    // lifetime near 20,000 frames -- ten times a leaf's, a fifth of a bole's
    // -- so a branch lies on the floor through a season of leaves coming and
    // going. That spread in *time* is as much of the graded outcome as the
    // chain is: the floor after a die-back is leaves, then branches, then
    // bare ground, rather than one uniform brown mat that never changes.
    decay_chance_damp: 0.08,
    decay_chance_dry: 0.01,
    // **No `reseed_chance`, so it defaults to 0.** Same argument `litter.ron`
    // makes: a rotting branch should not plant a tree, and deadwood cells are
    // produced in the hundreds by any die-back.
)
